is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The outage is not a WAN link failure. The affected component is router2’s control plane (FRRouting/RIP), not its WAN interface. Evidence: router2’s eth2 (WAN toward router1) is physically up (ethtool shows “Link detected: yes”, Speed 10G, Full duplex) and layer‑3 reachable; router2 successfully pings router1’s WAN IP 20.20.0.1 with 0% loss. Additionally, router1’s RIP table shows routes learned via 20.20.0.2, indicating the L2/L3 path is intact.

The symptom (Server Room 1.0.0.x and Internet Lab 128.168.0.x can’t reach 192.168.x.x and vice‑versa) is explained by router2 lacking routes to the 192.168.x.x departments. Router2’s routing table contains only connected networks (1.0.0.0/8, 128.168.0.0/16, 20.0.0.0/8). Pings from 1.0.0.5 and 128.168.0.2 to 192.168.3.2 return “Destination Net Unreachable” from 1.0.0.1/128.168.0.1 (router2), proving it has no route to 192.168.x.x. Direct cause: FRR is down on router2 (systemctl: frr.service failed; vtysh: “ripd is not running”), so router2 is not learning 192.168.x.x via RIP from router1.

Localization: router2, RIP daemon (ripd) within FRR; interfaces eth0/eth1/eth2 are up. Root cause: FRRouting (RIP) on router2 failed after maintenance, leaving no routes to 192.168.x.x. Corrective fix: restart/enable FRR on router2 and restore RIP configuration (advertise 1.0.0.0/8, 128.168.0.0/16, 20.0.0.0/8). Verify RIP adjacency with router1 and confirm 192.168.x.x routes appear on router2; then end‑to‑end pings should succeed. No WAN interface is down; router2 eth2 is healthy. 
=== END ===